WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.230
Film Courage: How does someone really know
they’re a writer?

00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:05.860
Maybe they just like the idea of being a writer
(the identity of it)?

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:09.300
Mark Sanderson, Screenwriter/Author: It’s
a good question (as always).

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:14.950
I’ve found with actors as well who I have
come in contact with that some people like

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:21.520
to play the role and others like to actually
do the work.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.590
You can see because when it starts getting
difficult those who want to play the role

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.279
go “Hmm…this is hard.”

00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:31.740
And then it becomes Well do you really want
to do this?

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.930
I always say writers write.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:37.840
Writers are a special breed, you know what
I mean?

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:43.899
I don’t know if it’s hard to explain but
you are the one up at 2 0’clock in the morning

00:00:43.899 --> 00:00:49.920
with passion and you can’t (to use a double
negative) not write.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:56.470
It’s something that is in your blood, it’s
in your DNA to be a creative person like that.

00:00:56.470 --> 00:01:00.790
So those people (like I said I used to work
in a restaurant as a waiter) and there were

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:04.400
some people who wanted to be actors and writers
as well.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:05.569
“I’m working on that thing.”

00:01:05.569 --> 00:01:06.770
And they never finished something.

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:11.939
That’s also another indication where you
can’t see it through where this person who

00:01:11.939 --> 00:01:16.319
writes, yes there’s a lot of fear involved
because it might be crap.

00:01:16.319 --> 00:01:20.060
You may not be able to level where you want
to be but you have to get through that to

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:26.659
get to the other side to master anything (martial
arts, whatever it is that you’re studying).

00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:33.899
So a lot of the people wanted to be “acteurs”
like the brooding “Yeah, you know I’m

00:01:33.899 --> 00:01:34.899
an actor.”

00:01:34.899 --> 00:01:35.899
Wearing the leather jacket.

00:01:35.899 --> 00:01:38.020
And you say “Well are you going on any auditions?”

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:39.020
“Well…no.”

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:40.020
“Are you taking classes?”

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.020
“Well…no.”

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.729
“You know, I have a good look.”

00:01:42.729 --> 00:01:51.200
Okay well that will only get you so far so
I think some people may try writing “Oh

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.880
I think I’d like to write a screenplay.”

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:54.369
Which is fantastic.

00:01:54.369 --> 00:02:01.770
It might not be maybe novel writing is better
suited or maybe both of them are not.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:05.599
It takes a big commitment so I think you find
your way.

00:02:05.599 --> 00:02:12.590
But for me since I was eleven I knew this
was kind of my calling making movies and things

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:17.230
when we were shooting films (short movies)
at eleven years old.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:24.370
I knew because nothing would stray me from
that.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:32.040
I think you either find out that it’s not
what you’re really passionate about or early

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:35.549
on you know it’s your passion early on and
nothing is going to stop you from it.

00:02:35.549 --> 00:02:40.760
So I think that’s what designates a writer
for the long haul.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:45.870
Film Courage: And the test for when things
are uncomfortable and it looks like it’s

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:46.870
not going to happen?

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:52.890
Mark: Yes, I always say failure is a test
to see how badly you want it.

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:59.150
And this life of a writer is filled with rejection,
failure and criticism and it’s not going

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:01.170
to go away even when you start working.

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:06.209
In fact I think the stakes are even higher
because then there is money involved and contracts

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:13.549
and pressure like I have to work under a deadline
and that was the first thing that was difficult

00:03:13.549 --> 00:03:17.799
for me when I started working professionally
is that you’re supposed to be creative under

00:03:17.799 --> 00:03:23.879
a deadline and how can you do that and some
when you’re working on your specs it’s

00:03:23.879 --> 00:03:24.879
open-ended right?

00:03:24.879 --> 00:03:29.260
You go Well I won’t work today or I’ll
go to the beach and when it becomes your job

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:33.069
it’s the job of any responsibility like
a job has to do.

00:03:33.069 --> 00:03:38.280
You clock in and your clock in time might
be three in the afternoon as long as you get

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:40.450
those pages done at a certain time.

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:42.170
Question for the Viewers: Did you write today?

